\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1}What Is an LED?}{3}{section.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {1.1}The LED's I--V Curve and the Load Line}{3}{subsection.1.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The LED I--V curve (red) and the load line (blue) for a $28\,\Omega $ resistor on a 5\,V supply. The load line runs from $(0,\,V_{CC}/R)$ on the current axis to $(V_{CC},\,0)$ on the voltage axis. The operating point (filled circle) is the unique intersection: the only combination of current and voltage that satisfies both the LED's physics and Ohm's law in the resistor simultaneously. Note that the operating point lies to the right of $V_f$ on the steep part of the curve, confirming that the LED is conducting. Moving to a smaller $R$ rotates the load line upward (steeper slope), raising both the current and the operating-point voltage slightly; a larger $R$ rotates it downward.}}{4}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:ivloadline}{{1}{4}{The LED I--V curve (red) and the load line (blue) for a $28\,\Omega $ resistor on a 5\,V supply. The load line runs from $(0,\,\Vcc /R)$ on the current axis to $(\Vcc ,\,0)$ on the voltage axis. The operating point (filled circle) is the unique intersection: the only combination of current and voltage that satisfies both the LED's physics and Ohm's law in the resistor simultaneously. Note that the operating point lies to the right of $\Vf $ on the steep part of the curve, confirming that the LED is conducting. Moving to a smaller $R$ rotates the load line upward (steeper slope), raising both the current and the operating-point voltage slightly; a larger $R$ rotates it downward}{figure.caption.1}{}}
